<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fatima al-Fihri</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 880</span> · <a href="/centuries/9th-century">9th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fatima al-Fihri was a woman from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> who settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> in the Idrisid period with her family. Using her inheritance, she founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-al-qarawiyyin">al-Qarawiyyin mosque</a> in 859, which grew into one of the leading centres of Islamic learning. Her sister Maryam is credited with founding the Andalusian mosque of Fez.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Little is known of Fatima's life beyond what the 14th-century historian Ibn Abi Zar' recorded in The Garden of Pages, which forms the basis of the traditional narrative. She is said to have been born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a>, possibly around 800, the daughter of a wealthy merchant, Muhammad al-Fihri al-Qayrawani. He came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> with his wife, his sister and his daughter as part of a larger migration of families from Kairouan in the early Idrisid period. When he died, Fatima inherited his wealth. She was also known as Umm al-Banin, the Mother of the Sons.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to Ibn Abi Zar', Fatima took no part in commerce and wished to devote her inheritance to a pious act. She bought a property in the centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> at high cost and laid the foundations of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-al-qarawiyyin">al-Qarawiyyin mosque</a> on the first day of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramadan">Ramadan</a> in the year 245 of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islamic-calendar">Islamic calendar</a>, 859 CE. The materials are said to have been quarried on the site, so that no doubt could fall on their source. Fatima fasted until the work was finished, then went in and prayed to thank God. The same tradition says her sister Maryam founded the mosque of the Andalusians in the district across the river at about the same time.</p><p>The mosque became the most important congregational mosque in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and one of the foremost intellectual centres of Islamic North Africa. Its teaching later became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-al-qarawiyyin">University of al-Qarawiyyin</a>, which took that form in 1963.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Fatima is said to have died around 880. Since she is first mentioned many centuries after her death, some modern historians doubt her existence. Roger Le Tourneau and Jonathan Bloom see the symmetry of two sisters founding the two great mosques of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> as too neat, and Bloom notes that no part of the present mosque is older than the tenth century. A foundation inscription found under plaster in the 20th century names Dawud ibn Idris as founder in 877 CE. Chafik Benchekroun argues it is the original inscription and that Fatima may be a figure of legend. Ian D. Morris finds no evidence that she founded the library or the university.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fatima al-Fihriya” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Al-Qarawiyin in Fes, founded by <b>Fatima al-Fihri</b> in 859, is held by UNESCO to be the world's oldest university; Nawal El Moutawakel became in 1984 the first woman from an Arab or Islamic country to win Olympic gold, the national team reached the World Cup semi-finals in 2022,…</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-morocco">Kingdom of Morocco · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/tunisia">Tunisia</a>. Died in Fez, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.68,10.1&amp;year=870" aria-label="Kairouan on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Fatima al-Fihri"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n30.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5698 4484h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5698 4484h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.68,10.1&amp;year=870">What was here in 870 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
